sections.s2_technical:
The `comparing` static method creates a Comparator from a key extractor function. It calls the extractor on both objects and compares the extracted keys using their natural order (Comparable).

```java
// Single field — ascending by symbol:
Comparator<Trade> bySymbol = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol);
trades.sort(bySymbol);
// HDFC, INFY, RELIANCE, TCS, WIPRO (alphabetical)
```

The `thenComparing` method adds a secondary sort key. It is a default method on Comparator that creates a new Comparator: when the primary comparison returns 0 (equal), it uses the secondary key.

```java
// Primary: symbol ascending. Secondary: amount descending.
Comparator<Trade> multiKey = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder());
// Within RELIANCE: 245050 before 123750 (descending)
// Within TCS: 176000 before 88750
```

The `reversed()` method flips the ENTIRE chain. Be careful — calling `.reversed()` at the end reverses ALL keys, not just the last one. To reverse only one specific key, pass `Comparator.reverseOrder()` as the second argument to `thenComparing`.

```java
// WRONG — reverses BOTH keys:
Comparator<Trade> wrong = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount)
    .reversed();
// Symbol descending AND amount descending!

// RIGHT — only amount descending:
Comparator<Trade> right = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getSymbol)
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder());
// Symbol ascending, amount descending within each symbol
```

The `nullsFirst` and `nullsLast` wrappers handle null keys gracefully. Without them, a null field causes NullPointerException during comparison.

```java
// Department might be null for some trades:
Comparator<Trade> nullSafe = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
    Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()));
// Non-null departments sorted alphabetically, null departments at the END

// nullsFirst: null departments at the BEGINNING
Comparator<Trade> nullFirst = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
    Comparator.nullsFirst(Comparator.naturalOrder()));
```

The `comparingInt`, `comparingLong`, and `comparingDouble` methods avoid autoboxing when extracting primitive fields.

```java
// ❌ Boxing: comparing extracts Integer object
Comparator<Trade> boxing = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getQuantity);

// ✅ No boxing: comparingInt extracts primitive int
Comparator<Trade> noBoxing = Comparator.comparingInt(Trade::getQuantity);
```

Why this is better than manual compare():

```java
// ❌ MANUAL: 25 lines, error-prone, hard to maintain
class TradeComparator implements Comparator<Trade> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Trade a, Trade b) {
        // Null checks for department
        if (a.getDepartment() == null && b.getDepartment() == null) {
            // fall through to secondary
        } else if (a.getDepartment() == null) {
            return 1;  // null last
        } else if (b.getDepartment() == null) {
            return -1;
        } else {
            int deptCompare = a.getDepartment().compareTo(b.getDepartment());
            if (deptCompare != 0) return deptCompare;
        }
        // Secondary: amount descending
        int amountCompare = b.getAmount().compareTo(a.getAmount());
        if (amountCompare != 0) return amountCompare;
        // Tertiary: tradeId ascending
        return a.getTradeId().compareTo(b.getTradeId());
    }
}

// ✅ FLUENT: 3 lines, readable, correct
Comparator<Trade> fluent = Comparator.comparing(Trade::getDepartment,
        Comparator.nullsLast(Comparator.naturalOrder()))
    .thenComparing(Trade::getAmount, Comparator.reverseOrder())
    .thenComparing(Trade::getTradeId);
```

